# shm-coherence: the ONE live-worker leg of the cross-process shm lens.
#
# Every other test of `fanout`, `quiesce` and `zone_invariant` is a unit test in
# assert_test.c over synthetic readings -- pure functions, no master, no fork,
# no accept path, no mapped zone. This file is where those directives meet four
# real nginx workers, and it exists to prove the parts a unit fixture cannot:
# that a `fanout` leg really is answered by a worker whose pid the probe can
# read, that consecutive fresh connections really do land on DIFFERENT workers,
# and that the coverage oracle really does count them.
#
# worker_processes 4 is in nginx.conf and PROBER_ALLOW_MULTIWORKER=1 is in
# ./env; the two must stay together (see ./env and multi-worker.rule).
#
#
# WHAT THIS SCENARIO ASSERTS
# --------------------------
#   - `fanout` executes: N legs, each judged against the case's own expects, so
#     a worker that answers one leg differently is a finding in its own right.
#   - The coverage oracle: at least min_workers DISTINCT answering pids, and
#     INCOMPLETE COVERAGE FAILS. This is the load-bearing assertion. Without it
#     the whole lens could pass having sampled one worker N times.
#   - `quiesce`: a named counter read to two consecutive equal values inside its
#     timeout. Expiry FAILS; it never degrades to a skip or a sleep.
#
#
# WHAT THIS SCENARIO DELIBERATELY DOES *NOT* ASSERT -- AND WHY
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# No `zone_invariant` line appears below, in any of its three forms
# (`coherent`, `at_rest`, `monotonic`). That is a decision, not an oversight,
# and reversing it without the missing piece would make this file worse.
#
# The reference module never allocates from the shm slab on a request path.
# Measured live (nginx 1.31.4, four workers): `zone.slab_reqs` stays 0 across a
# full sweep. Serving an HTTP request allocates from the per-request POOL; the
# only `ngx_slab_alloc` call in the reference probe is the canary arm, which is
# arm-driven and no request reaches. t/module's reference module also registers
# no `zone_render` hook by design, so no module counter is rendered either.
#
# Against a zone nothing mutates, every reading is the same number, and:
#   - `coherent <field>`  is satisfied by any constant;
#   - `at_rest <field> == N` is satisfied by a field that was never anything
#     but N;
#   - `monotonic <field>` is satisfied because a constant sequence never
#     decreases.
# All three would be GREEN here, and green for a reason that has nothing to do
# with whether their comparisons are correct -- a run that proves nothing while
# reporting success, which is the exact failure class this harness exists to
# catch. Asserting them here would buy a passing line and spend the trust.
#
# This is true of all three forms EQUALLY. None of `coherent`, `at_rest` or
# `monotonic` is better covered than the others against this module, and the
# per-leg probe reads a `fanout` sweep takes do not change that for any of
# them -- a probe read does not allocate from the zone, so more of them is not
# more coverage.
#
# ROUTES ALREADY TRIED AND WHY THEY FAIL (do not re-attempt these on this
# module without new information):
#   - Driving `monotonic` off `connections.free`
#     (`ngx_cycle->free_connection_n`). Rejected: it is per-worker and RESETS
#     to baseline between fanout legs, since each leg's connection closes
#     before the next leg's probe read. Measured live, 5/5 consecutive runs:
#     `fanout 24 2` + `monotonic connections.free` GREEN, 24/24 responses from
#     4 distinct workers. Never a falling sequence.
#   - Pairing `open_conns` with `zone_invariant` to hold connections open
#     across the probe sweep. Rejected: `open_conns` executes strictly AFTER
#     the zone_invariant block in `run_case()` even on the same case (see
#     collect_zone_readings and the open_conns ordering comment in prober.c),
#     so its held connections cannot exist yet when these readings are taken.
#   - Falling back to `eval_zone_monotonic`'s other red branches (the `n < 2`
#     refusal, the underflow/wrap sentinel). Rejected: `n < 2` is unreachable
#     because the fanout parser floor is already 2, and the sentinel branch
#     prints its own message instead of the decrease line a driver would need
#     to match -- neither gives a live case something to assert against here.
#
# The forms are implemented, load-checked and unit-tested (assert_test.c drives
# each comparison against synthetic readings, including the reds); they are
# simply not asserted from THIS scenario. They get their live coverage when a
# consumer zone with a mutating request path is wired -- cache-turbo's
# `varidx_inflight` -- where each form becomes falsifiable.
#
#
# WHAT THIS LENS IS, AND IS NOT
# -----------------------------
# IS: a detector of race CONSEQUENCES -- a leaked counter, a diverged view
# between two workers, an underflow that wrapped.
#
# IS NOT: a race detector. It never observes an interleaving, only a broken
# invariant afterwards. A run that is green means no observed consequence in
# the sample it took, not that the code is race-free. Claiming otherwise would
# recreate the vacuous-green problem this lens was built to replace (helgrind
# and TSan model pthread races inside ONE address space; nginx workers are
# fork()ed processes sharing an mmap under ngx_shmtx, so neither can see this
# race class at all).
#
#
# WHY `fanout 24` AND NOT A SMALLER NUMBER
# ----------------------------------------
# Worker sampling is probabilistic: nothing lets a client choose which worker
# accepts its connection, and a run CAN legitimately put every request on one
# worker. Measured on this box, four workers, sequential fresh connections:
#
#   fanout 8   -> 2 distinct workers in 7 of 8 runs, 3 in 1 of 8. Never 4.
#   fanout 24  -> 4 distinct workers in 4 of 4 runs; >= 3 in 7 of 7.
#
# So the width, not the bound, is what buys reliability. min_workers stays at
# the meaningful 2 -- observed floor 4, required 2, three workers of margin.
#
# The temptation when this flakes is to lower min_workers to 1. Do not. A
# min_workers of 1 is a disarmed oracle and the parser refuses it at load time
# (eval_fanout_coverage rejects it too, belt and braces): every fanout ever
# written would then pass having sampled a single worker N times, and the one
# assertion this scenario exists for would assert nothing. If a box cannot
# reach 2 distinct workers at this width, widen the fanout or investigate the
# accept path -- the bound is not the adjustable part.

name    fanout across four workers reaches distinct workers, every leg served
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~OK
# Without pid_may_change the strict same-worker oracle would redden the very
# rotation this case is built to observe. The coverage oracle below is what
# keeps that relaxation from being a free pass: multi-worker.rule degrades to
# "same master", this case MEASURES the spread and fails when it is too narrow.
pid_may_change
fanout  24 2

# A second case at the same width. Not redundant: the first case's spread could
# in principle be an artifact of a cold accept path on the freshly forked worker
# set, and a case run after the server has served traffic samples a warm one.
# Both must reach the bound.
name    a warm worker set still spreads across distinct workers
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~OK
pid_may_change
fanout  24 2

# quiesce on a live server. `zone.slab_reqs` is the field: on the reference
# module it is a constant 0 (see above), so this case asserts the DIRECTIVE
# works end to end against a real probe document -- it polls, it reads two
# consecutive equal values, and it returns inside its timeout rather than
# expiring. A quiesce that expires FAILS the case, so an unreadable field or a
# probe that stopped answering reddens here rather than passing quietly.
#
# It is paired with a fanout because the parser requires it: a zone_invariant
# needs a fanout, and a quiesce needs SOMETHING to read the settled state --
# the fanout's per-leg probe snapshots are that reader. A quiesce nothing
# observes is a sleep, and the parser refuses one.
name    quiesce settles on a live zone counter, then the fanout still spreads
from    127.0.0.1
send    GET / HTTP/1.1\r\n
send    Host: prober\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n
expect  status=200
expect  body~OK
pid_may_change
# The reader the parser demands, and not a vacuous one: -1 is the documented
# "zone mapped but slab pool not yet initialised" sentinel, so `>= 0` fails
# closed on an uninitialised pool rather than letting the quiesce settle on a
# field the probe could not actually read.
probe   zone.slab_reqs >= 0
quiesce zone.slab_reqs 5000
fanout  24 2
